\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\title{Symmetries of graded spin fields}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{A coordinate inversion or a conjugation of coefficients can
carry a Fourier mode to a different momentum block. How does
the action on a rotation within the block change?
Transport of the stabilizer action (\ref{fdip-0175}) tracks the degree
using the two chosen generators and whether the field map conjugates
scalars. What does the centered symbol say about the spin value?
For a nonzero axial symbol in odd dimension, the transverse-product
eigenvalue depends on the volume sign, the signs of the axial
coefficient and symbol eigenvalue, and the factor fixed by the axis
order and volume normalization (\ref{fdip-017B}). In even
dimension the volume grading exchanges opposite symbol eigenspaces
(\ref{fdip-017D}).}

\p{When does a conjugation with a prescribed direction sign exist?
The ordered traces of products of spin directions decide existence
(\ref{fdip-017K}). The simple spin types and their multiplicities
determine which scalar squares can occur (\ref{fdip-017P}). On fields,
charge conjugation of the adjoint also conjugates the represented
links (\ref{fdip-017R}). When can a map exchange the two parts of a
graded coupling? For orthogonal sign spaces with unitary group actions,
an isometric exchange exists exactly when both sign spaces admit
bijective intertwiners with their opposites at the target, both linear
or both conjugate-linear for the same change of group (\ref{fdip-018D}).
For a specified
inversion or charge conjugation, covariance of an even coefficient
requires both of its blocks to match those at the transformed data
(\ref{fdip-018H}, \ref{fdip-018I}).}

\p{When the time map uses the same charge conjugation as the
intervening field map, applying spatial inversion, charge conjugation
and the time map in that order gives unitary total inversion
(\ref{fdip-019E}). It transports the massive Wilson kernel to the
inverted links and preserves its determinant (\ref{fdip-019F}). The
real-trace plaquette sum gives an invariant gauge factor when composed
with any real function (\ref{fdip-019H}). For a connection probability invariant under both
time reflection and total inversion, the reflected pairing (\ref{fdip-019N})
passes from one closed time half to the other.}

\transclude{fdip-0171}
\transclude{fdip-017F}
\transclude{fdip-0188}
\transclude{fdip-019A}
